<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The shader graph: a node library of maths, texture, vertex-data and master nodes over the
            reusable node-graph framework, compiling to Raven source that the same compiler a
            hand-written shader goes through then type-checks.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>editor;shader-graph;node-graph;raven;shaders;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <!--
          CA1051 (no visible instance fields) is the one rule this assembly cannot keep. A node's
          ports *are* its fields — that is the design in docs/plan/11, and it is what lets a node
          read as `[Input] public DynamicVector A;` and an Emit read as `$"{A} + {B}"`. Properties
          would work and would mean the generator wrote a backing store and the author wrote `A.Value`
          everywhere, which is a worse declaration for no benefit: these types are compile-time
          scaffolding built and thrown away by the compiler, never a public API anyone holds.
        -->
        <!--
            #821's ratchet, and this one is the case where the answer is nothing: forced on across
            this project's whole reference closure the tree reports thirty diagnostics and **not one
            of them is in this assembly**. So the flag lands as a latch rather than a repair — the
            forty-odd node classes here carry the densest `<see cref>` prose in the editor, and
            keeping them checked is worth more than the day they were found clean.

            CS1591 and CS1573 are suppressed for the reason `Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.csproj` gives:
            a node is compile-time scaffolding and not a packaged API, so "this public member has no
            comment" is not a question worth asking of it, while the rules that catch a *lie* are.
        -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1051</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.csproj" />

        <!--
          The preview renderer's three, and nothing else: a device to make a target on, the effect
          model that says what a compiled variant binds, and the in-process Raven compiler the editor
          already resolves its own variants through. The node library and the emitter are still
          reachable with none of them — `ShaderGraphPreview` compiles a sub-expression to text on its
          own, which is what the compiler tests exercise.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Graphics\Vixen.Graphics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Shaders\Vixen.Shaders.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Tools\Vixen.ShaderCompiler\Vixen.ShaderCompiler.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the material model, for the one thing a graph cannot do on its own: say what it is as a
          material. `Master/Surface` emits an `IMaterialSurface`, which is exactly what an
          `IMaterialFeature` names, so turning a compiled graph into the feature a `.vxmat` composes
          is a mechanical read of `ShaderGraphSource` — and it belongs beside the compiler that
          produced those names rather than in whichever assembly happens to import a file. Editor to
          Core, which is the direction CheckArchitecture allows; nothing here opens a device or knows
          about a project.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Rendering\Vixen.Rendering.csproj" />

        <!--
          The node generator, over this assembly's own [Node] classes. Analyzers do not flow through
          a ProjectReference, so every assembly declaring nodes references it directly — which is
          also what a third-party plugin does, and the reason the arrangement is worth keeping plain.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          ⚠ The library a node preview is compiled beside — [#510](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/510).
          The preview renderer handed Raven one source, so a node calling `ComputeColor.ValueNoise`
          failed to bind with RVN2010 and five shipped nodes therefore declared no preview at all.
          `FromSources` takes a *set* of texts and makes them one compilation, so the fix is the
          library's own files in the same list — not a compiled `.rvnlib`, not a second copy of the
          arithmetic.

          The four are a closure and not a selection, exactly as `Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph`'s are:
          `Material/ComputeColor.rvn` is the node vocabulary, it imports `Vixen.Shaders.Core`, and
          `Core/Random.rvn` spells `Math.SphericalToCartesian` and `Const.TwoPi`, so `Core/Math.rvn`
          comes with it. ⚠ Every file in a compilation is bound whether the preview calls into it or
          not, so a set stopping one edge short fails on every previewed node at once rather than on
          the one that called.

          Embedded from the library's own path, so there is one copy of these functions in the
          repository and editing the library edits what a preview compiles against.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\Raven\Library\Core\ColorSpaces.rvn"
                          LogicalName="Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph.Prelude.Core.ColorSpaces.rvn" />
        <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\Raven\Library\Core\Math.rvn"
                          LogicalName="Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph.Prelude.Core.Math.rvn" />
        <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\Raven\Library\Core\Random.rvn"
                          LogicalName="Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph.Prelude.Core.Random.rvn" />
        <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\Raven\Library\Material\ComputeColor.rvn"
                          LogicalName="Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph.Prelude.Material.ComputeColor.rvn" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph.Tests" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **Two production assemblies, because a diagnostic family is not per assembly** —
            [#982](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/982). `ShaderGraphDiagnostics` declares the
            whole `SG` family and `SG0000`/`SG0100` are reported from
            `Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors.ShaderGraphDocument`, which already has a `ProjectReference`
            here. The alternative was making the declaration public, which buys a `docs/DocsExempt`
            line and a permanent API surface for nine constants nothing outside the editor may spell;
            the seam is narrower than the export.
        -->
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors" />
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
